# Static-asset response headers (celld reads this file with the deployment).
#
# celld serves an asset with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` and no `Last-Modified`, so a browser revalidates every file
# on every load. The docs' remedy is a `_headers` rule that gives a
# content-hashed file a long cache: Vite emits every chunk and stylesheet as
# `/assets/<name>-<hash>.<ext>`, so the name changes whenever the bytes do and
# the file can be cached forever.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Cross-origin isolation for the whole control UI. Every document, module and
# worker script needs `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` +
# `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp`, because Nodepod's workspace and
# the TypeScript language service pass `SharedArrayBuffer` to workers, which a
# browser only exposes in a cross-origin-isolated agent cluster. The Vite dev
# server sets the same three headers on every response (`vite.config.ts`:
# `server.headers` plus a top-of-stack middleware for the responses Vite does
# not pass them to) so dev and prod match.
#
# `require-corp` (not `credentialless`): the UI loads no third-party
# subresource — every script, stylesheet, font and image is same-origin (the
# avatars are initials, the docs links are plain `<a>` navigations) — so
# nothing legitimate needs the looser `credentialless` escape hatch.
# `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin` keeps our own assets from being
# embedded elsewhere, the counterpart to the isolation above.
#
# celld applies every matching rule and combines repeated header names, so
# these live in their own `/*` block, separate from the `/assets/*` cache rule.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
